    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 6z+10\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 6z+{6}^{2}}_{\u23df}-\\,{6}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-6{)}^{2}-{6}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-6{)}^{2}-26.\n$$",
