      "title": "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 9y-48\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${9}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-48$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 9y+{9}^{2}}_{\u23df}-\\,{9}^{2}-48\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-9{)}^{2}-{9}^{2}-48\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-9{)}^{2}-129.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=9$ and $q=-129.$",
